LM-006/s:why-hybrid.full:
). AWS lists hybrid post-quantum key exchange as launched on FIPS and non-FIPS endpoints for AWS KMS, AWS Payment Cryptography and the AWS CloudHSM control plane. AWS pages differ on the edges, so check the endpoint and Region you use: the
Transitioning a massive network to brand-new, cutting-edge cryptographic algorithms introduces risk. "Hybrid Cryptography" is the strategy of combining a highly trusted classical algorithm (like X25519) with a new quantum-safe algorithm (like ML-KEM) simultaneously.
Hybrid cryptography combines classical and PQC algorithms so that security holds even if one component is broken

LM-008/s:pqc-tls.full:
example.com { tls { # TLS 1.3 only protocols tls1.3 # Classical key exchange only (removes the default hybrid group) curves x25519 secp256r1 } reverse_proxy localhost:8080 }
example.com { tls { # TLS 1.3 only protocols tls1.3 # Hybrid PQC + classical key exchange # x25519mlkem768: ML-KEM-768 combined with X25519 (Caddy's default) # Falls back to x25519 and secp256r1 for non-PQC clients curves x25519mlkem768 x25519 secp256r1 } reverse_proxy localhost:8080 }
# Note: Caddy has no ML-KEM-only key exchange group (no mlkem768 or # mlkem1024 setting). The strictest option is the hybrid group alone. example.com { tls { # TLS 1.3 only protocols tls1.3 # Hybrid group only — no classical-only fallback # WARNING: clients without X25519MLKEM768 support will fail to connect curves x25519mlkem768 } reverse_proxy localhost:8080 }
Compare overhead of classical, hybrid, and PQC TLS configurations quantitatively
TLS protects nearly all web traffic; the 1.3 handshake is the prerequisite for every hybrid-PQC rollout you'll plan.
Key exchange approaches: - Classical (ECDH) — X25519, P-256, P-384; fast with small keys (~32 bytes); quantum-vulnerable - PQC (ML-KEM) — ML-KEM-512/768/1024; quantum-resistant; larger keys (~1,184 bytes for ML-KEM-768) - Hybrid — X25519MLKEM768 (RFC 10024, TLS NamedGroup codepoint `0x11EC`) combines X25519 and ML-KEM-768; already deployed in Chrome and Firefox; secure even if one algorithm is broken
