Compile with the OASIS PKCS#11 v3.2 headers and an implementation supporting CKM_ML_DSA_KEY_PAIR_GEN. CKA_PARAMETER_SET takes the numeric CK_ML_DSA_PARAMETER_SET_TYPE value CKP_ML_DSA_65, not a name string; the private key inherits this selection from the public template. Check the returned CK_RV before using handles. The caller initializes its chosen provider, opens and authenticates a read/write sample-token session, then destroys owned objects, logs out, closes the session and finalizes. CKA_TOKEN requests token storage and CKA_SENSITIVE restricts private-value reads; opaque handles alone do not establish hardware residency or non-extractability. Evaluate these capabilities and failure behavior in your production tool.
Lightweight API — direct class instantiation, no provider registration.
Same Signature engine calls as RSA or ECDSA; what changes is the algorithm name, the key type and the ~3.3 KB signature size your storage and protocols must carry.
#include <openssl/evp.h> #include <openssl/crypto.h> // Pure ML-DSA-65: no external digest. Only 1 means a signature was produced. // On success the caller owns *signature and releases it with OPENSSL_free. int sample_sign_mldsa65(EVP_PKEY *key, const unsigned char *message, size_t message_length, unsigned char **signature, size_t *signature_length) { if (signature != NULL) *signature = NULL; if (signature_length != NULL) *signature_length = 0; if (signature == NULL || signature_length == NULL) return 0; if (key == NULL || message == NULL || !EVP_PKEY_is_a(key, "ML-DSA-65")) return 0; EVP_MD_CTX *ctx = EVP_MD_CTX_new(); unsigned char *buffer = NULL; size_t length = 0; int ok = 0; if (ctx == NULL || EVP_DigestSignInit_ex(ctx, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, key, NULL) != 1 || EVP_DigestSign(ctx, NULL, &length, message, message_length) != 1 || length == 0) goto done; buffer = OPENSSL_malloc(length); if (buffer == NULL || EVP_DigestSign(ctx, buffer, &length, message, message_length) != 1 || length == 0) goto done; *signature = buffer; *signature_length = length; buffer = NULL; ok = 1; done: OPENSSL_free(buffer); EVP_MD_CTX_free(ctx); return ok; }
// PKCS#11 signing with ML-DSA CK_MECHANISM mech = {CKM_ML_DSA, NULL, 0}; C_SignInit(session, &mech, hPrivKey); CK_ULONG sigLen; C_Sign(session, msg, msgLen, NULL, &sigLen); CK_BYTE_PTR sig = malloc(sigLen); C_Sign(session, msg, msgLen, sig, &sigLen);
HSM performs the signing operation internally. Whether the key can leave the token depends on its CKA_EXTRACTABLE/CKA_SENSITIVE attributes and token policy.
// PKCS#11 v3.2 KEM encapsulation CK_MECHANISM mech = { CKM_ML_KEM, NULL, 0 }; CK_ATTRIBUTE deriveTmpl[] = { {CKA_CLASS, &secretClass, sizeof(CK_OBJECT_CLASS)}, {CKA_KEY_TYPE, &aesType, sizeof(CK_KEY_TYPE)}, {CKA_VALUE_LEN, &aesLen, sizeof(CK_ULONG)} }; CK_ULONG ctLen; CK_OBJECT_HANDLE hDerivedKey; C_EncapsulateKey(session, &mech, hPubKey, deriveTmpl, 3, NULL, &ctLen, &hDerivedKey); CK_BYTE_PTR ct = malloc(ctLen); C_EncapsulateKey(session, &mech, hPubKey, deriveTmpl, 3, ct, &ctLen, &hDerivedKey);
C_EncapsulateKey is new in PKCS#11 v3.2. Returns both ciphertext and a derived key handle.
Returns boolean — no exception on invalid signature.
#include <openssl/evp.h> // A fresh context per check: 1 = valid, 0 = invalid, other values = error. int sample_verify_mldsa65(EVP_PKEY *key, const unsigned char *message, size_t message_length, const unsigned char *signature, size_t signature_length) { if (key == NULL || message == NULL || signature == NULL || !EVP_PKEY_is_a(key, "ML-DSA-65")) return -1; EVP_MD_CTX *ctx = EVP_MD_CTX_new(); int result = -1; if (ctx != NULL && EVP_DigestVerifyInit_ex(ctx, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, key, NULL) == 1) result = EVP_DigestVerify(ctx, signature, signature_length, message, message_length); EVP_MD_CTX_free(ctx); return result; }
Use the one-shot EVP_DigestVerify call for pure ML-DSA. Only return value 1 establishes a valid signature; 0 means invalid, and other values indicate an error, which can also include a malformed signature. Both the public key and message must belong to the intended check; changing either requires verification again.
Symmetric encryption pattern is the same regardless of how the key was derived (classical or PQC KEM).
EVP_Encrypt* API handles all symmetric ciphers. Tag retrieval is GCM-specific.

